// @check
// @error no `n` on `T` — `i32` is not a bound
// @error `i32` is not a bound — a bound is a type with method members (`type i32 = { m(): R }`) or an inline `{ m(): R }`
// THE MEMBER FLOOR'S WHOLE DOMAIN IS "THE ANNOTATION IS NOT A BOUND" — `boundObjRootOf`
// answering -1. An UNBOUNDED `<T>` is an entirely open hole routed onto inference before
// this rung (D1431), and a REAL bound missing the member is answered by
// `error-strict-body-member-not-in-bound.vl`'s rung, which names the bound and what it
// grants. Neither reaches here.
//
// TWO DIAGNOSTICS, AND THEY DIVIDE THE WORK. The member sentence says which read failed and
// that the annotation is not a bound, at the read. The instantiation's sentence carries the
// RULE and the spelling that satisfies it. The member sentence must assert nothing further:
// it used to say `i32` "is not a type with members" — true here and FALSE of the
// field-member alias in the sibling file — and before that it told this author to declare
// the bound they had already declared.
//
// `i32` is the sub-case with no members to have. The sibling
// `error-member-on-field-member-alias-bound.vl` is the one that has them.
function f<T: i32>(x: T): i32 {
  x.n
}

print(f(1))
